1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,099
Same idea as last time, but in Python — so you can lean on Python's libraries.

2
00:00:03,099 --> 00:00:06,000
It's almost identical, with one extra requirement you need to know about.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,646
New tool, same as before, but choose Python for the body.

4
00:00:08,646 --> 00:00:13,519
Write a normal Python function — here, one that calls a real HTTP API with urllib and returns the result.

5
00:00:13,519 --> 00:00:17,000
Save it, enable it, and the model can call it just like the JavaScript one.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,283
The one extra requirement: Python tools need Python available on your system PATH on the desktop app.

7
00:00:21,283 --> 00:00:26,074
If a Python tool silently does nothing, that's the first thing to check — the app couldn't find Python to run it.

8
00:00:26,074 --> 00:00:29,000
JavaScript tools have no such dependency; they run in the app itself.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,024
One convenience, with a caveat.

10
00:00:30,024 --> 00:00:34,417
The app tries to auto-detect the parameter schema from your function's signature and docstring, which saves you writing JSON by hand.

11
00:00:34,417 --> 00:00:38,712
But check it — auto-detection isn't perfect, and a wrong schema means the model calls your tool with the wrong shape of arguments.

12
00:00:38,712 --> 00:00:40,000
A quick look saves a confusing failure.

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,586
You can now extend the agent in two languages.

14
00:00:41,586 --> 00:00:45,517
Last in the tools module: letting the model enable tools itself, and the habit of reading exactly what a tool did.

15
00:00:45,517 --> 00:00:46,000
See you there.

